[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) holds every report about a wrong date since April. The desk merged
them on the subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) and marked the
ticket known. The reports do not share one cause. Read by tenant, by channel and by body, they
show separate problems. Each problem now has its own ticket.

<Callout kind="warn">
The fix queued for #4471 fixes the parser only. The other problems stay after it ships.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

## parser: day and month change places

The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the day first. So a
date the tenant writes as 03/10 comes out as 10 March: "Customer booked 3 October and got an
invite for 10 March." This is the problem #4471 describes, and the queued fix is for it.

## hint: the booking-v3 form asks for the month first

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first tenants on booking-v3
type the month first, as the hint tells them, and the form reads the date day first. The report
looks the same as a parser report. Only the tenant's form and the channel (`form`) tell them
apart. A parser fix does not fix it, because the input itself is in the wrong order.

## one-day-move: not a swap

Some reports describe a date one day off, or two recipients who see different days for the
same meeting: "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting. We held
it twice." A day/month swap cannot do that. The desk already noted these reports in
`desk-notes.json`, and the cause table on #4471 files them under `parser`, because it sorts by
tenant. This page counts them the same way: under their cause, and again in their own row. The
cause is not known yet. The ticket asks engineering to check time zones and
daylight saving time.

## For the desk

- Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser. The `tagged as` column shows that it
  disagrees with the tenant. Place a report by its tenant, its channel and its body, not by its tag.
- A subject line tells you what a person saw, not why it happened. Here every problem in the
  table shares the same "wrong date" subjects. Decision 0001 accepted that ("engineering merges
  them later"), but a ticket marked known hid the other problems behind the fix for the parser.
  Split a big ticket by cause before you mark it known.

